course: dictionary
lesson: 7826
title: Quite: fairly, fully or a lot?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Quite is a small word with more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it and try each meaning in the sentence.
explain: «Active tardigrades are quite fragile.» Here quite means fairly: they are fragile, but not very. Try the swap: «fairly fragile» still makes sense.
explain: «There was quite a crowd at the gate.» Quite a means a lot of, so this means there were a lot of people. When your teacher writes «not quite» on your work, it means almost right but not right yet.
fact: Quite is an adverb, so it often goes before an adjective, like quite good, or another adverb, like quite well.
fact: «Quite» can mean fairly, more than a little but not very: «quite hungry» is hungry, but not very hungry.
fact: «Quite» can mean completely or fully, as in «quite right».
fact: «Not quite» means almost, but not right yet.
fact: British speakers often use «quite» to mean fairly, and Americans often use it to mean very.
exercise: «Active tardigrades are quite fragile.» In this sentence, quite means | Completely, totally | Not at all | Very, with no doubt | *Fairly, not very
exercise: Your teacher writes «not quite» on your sum. What does it mean? | You have finished early | *Your answer is almost right, but not right yet | Your answer is perfect and fully right | Your answer is wrong, so start again
exercise: «There was quite a crowd at the gate.» What does quite a crowd mean? | A few people only | *A lot of people | A crowd of animals | Nobody at all
test: «I'm quite hungry after school.» Quite here means | Not at all | Completely, every bit | Never | *Fairly, more than a little
test: «I'm not quite ready.» Quite means fully or completely, so this sentence means | I am totally ready | *I am almost ready, but not fully there yet | I never get ready | I am ready to go right now
exam: «BSL, Auslan and New Zealand Sign Language belong to one family, so their signers understand each other quite well.» Quite well means | Very badly | *Fairly well | Perfectly, with no mistakes | Not at all
